   {
    "id": "q3",
    "text": "Which counter takes a wrong register number combined with a write to a read-only register, and does an unknown register get the type/length/register/access counter?",
    "default": "Both go to the type, length, register or access error counter (the seventh).",
    "reason": "The status layout lists a single counter for these.",
    "answer": "Both go to the type, length, register or access error counter (the seventh).",
    "status": "defaulted",
    "by": "oracle",
    "evidence": null
   },
   {
    "id": "q4",
    "text": "Is a packet with a bad type or wrong length counted as using up its sequence number?",
    "default": "No: those rules come before the sequence rule, so the number is not used up.",
    "reason": "Judging order puts them before the sequence rule.",
    "answer": "No. A packet that fails the type or length check is refused before the sequence rule, so it never passes that rule and does not use up its sequence number.",
    "status": "answered",
    "by": "oracle",
    "evidence": "Checks in order: long, short, bad CRC (silent drops), type, length, sequence, then register number and access; a command that passes the sequence rule consumes its number."
   },
   {
    "id": "q5",
    "text": "Does a telemetry-start with the reply count as a command carried out, and what is the telemetry frame count in a status reply?",
    "default": "Yes, counted when status OK; frame count is the number of frames held since reset.",
    "reason": "The request does not state it.",
    "answer": "Not specified for the first part: the spec does not say whether a telemetry-start command counts as carried out. For the second part, the spec gives the status reply's telemetry frame count (16 bit) and says the frame count changes only when a frame is held and only reset clears it. That supports 'frames held since reset', but the spec does not say 'when status OK'.",
    "status": "answered",
    "by": "oracle",
    "evidence": "Frame sequence and frame count change only when a frame is held and only reset clears them."
   },
